// [over.ics.rank]/3.2.3 with [over.match.best]/2: binding a reference is
// an *identity* conversion, exactly like copying into a by-value
// parameter, so for a bare lvalue argument neither `f(int)` nor
// `f(int&)` has a better conversion sequence than the other and the call
// is ambiguous. clang++-22 -std=c++26 rejects this program for the same
// reason.
//
// This case previously asserted the opposite -- that "by-value vs.
// by-reference is a separate, orthogonal axis" on which an lvalue picks
// `f(int&)` and a prvalue picks `f(int)` -- and attributed it to
// "ch05 §5.10". No such clause exists: chapter 05 of docs/spec is
// `05-unions-and-packed-layout.md`, whose only sections are §9.1-§9.4 on
// union types. Nothing in the spec modifies [over.ics.rank], so the
// ordinary C++ rule applies unchanged and the old behaviour was an
// unauthorised deviation that silently called a different function than
// C++ specifies.
int f(int x) {
    return x * 2;
}

int f(int& x) {
    x = x + 1;
    return x * 3;
}

int main() {
    int y = 10;
    return f(y);
}
